The block-bootstrap interval for the cosine row is at most $[0.30,0.44]$, wider than the independent interval. Why does serial correlation widen intervals?
\end{checkpoint}
\checkanswer{Correlated neighbors carry partly redundant information, so 100 correlated levels behave like fewer independent ones. Independent resampling ignores this redundancy and underestimates the variability; resampling blocks keeps neighbors together and recovers the larger spread (Chapter~\ref{ch:controls}).}

patches.new:
The block-bootstrap interval for the cosine row is at most $[0.30,0.44]$, slightly wider than the independent interval. Does serial correlation always widen bootstrap intervals?
\end{checkpoint}
\checkanswer{No. The effect depends on the sign of the dependence and on the statistic. For a sample mean, positive serial correlation enlarges the true sampling spread and negative correlation shrinks it, and the fluctuations here are negatively correlated at short lags. For the rms, the relevant dependence is that of the squared errors $e_n^2$: if errors of similar size cluster in runs, independent resampling understates the variability, even when the signed fluctuations alternate. The paper observes only a slight widening. The block bootstrap is the safer choice because it makes no independence assumption (Chapter~\ref{ch:controls}).}

\emph{Status.} Steps 4--8 are a heuristic estimate resting on five approximations:
\begin{itemize}
\item the Wigner surmise replaces the exact spacing distribution of the zeros;
\item $d_n$ is treated as stationary, although its variance grows slowly with $n$;
\item $\gap$ is treated as constant between neighbors;
\item $\sigma$ is borrowed from the holdout 401--500 (with the in-range value $0.2438$ for zeros 1--400, the same formula gives $\rho_1\approx-0.50$, so the magnitude is sensitive to this choice);
\item $\rho_2=-0.10$ is chosen by hand.
\end{itemize}
The robust point is the sign: the spacing variance $0.178$ exceeds $2\sigma^2$ for either choice of $\sigma$, so the lag-1 autocorrelation must be negative. The illustrative $-0.43$ is of the same order as the paper's measured $-0.26$ to $-0.41$. This is not a test, because the paper's values are measured on held-out subsets with training neighbors. Problem~15 asks you to measure the actual autocorrelations; the paper states only that they are negative at lags 1--3.
